    example: program('byte[] input = new byte[22];\njava.util.Arrays.fill(input, (byte) 0x0b);\nbyte[] salt = java.util.HexFormat.of().parseHex("000102030405060708090a0b0c");\nbyte[] context = java.util.HexFormat.of().parseHex("f0f1f2f3f4f5f6f7f8f9");\nbyte[] output = derive(input, salt, context, 42);\nSystem.out.println(output.length);', 'static byte[] derive(byte[] input, byte[] salt, byte[] context, int length) throws java.security.GeneralSecurityException {\n  var spec = javax.crypto.spec.HKDFParameterSpec.ofExtract().addIKM(input).addSalt(salt).thenExpand(context, length);\n  return javax.crypto.KDF.getInstance("HKDF-SHA256").deriveData(spec);\n}'), output: '42',
    trace: ['Use the published nonsecret input, salt and context', 'Extract and expand with HKDF-SHA256', 'Return 42 derived bytes without printing key material'],
    followUp: 'Should HKDF directly hash a user password for storage? No. Password storage needs an appropriate password-hashing design and work factor. This HKDF example assumes suitable input keying material and demonstrates derivation, not password authentication.',
